{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":36551969,"ns":0,"title":"Arithmetic number","extract":"In number theory, an arithmetic number is an integer for which the average of its positive divisors is also an integer.  For instance, 6 is an arithmetic number because the average of its divisors is\n\n  \n    \n      \n        \n          \n            \n              1\n              +\n              2\n              +\n              3\n              +\n              6\n            \n            4\n          \n        \n        =\n        3\n        ,\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle {\\frac {1+2+3+6}{4}}=3,}\n  \n\nwhich is also an integer.","description":"Integer where the average of its positive divisors is also an integer","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}